{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792692653,"nonce":"1PRUEWgdfq8CrYl9YWoPoF8xOoE=","message":"Controls and implementation scope:\n\nBefore implementation, both new GPU controls failed: an unclipped half-red pixel at (25,25) outside a cubic circle and at (14,14) outside a sheared multi-contour path. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-path-negative.log. After implementation, temporarily returning no rows from the new raw-image path capture made all six app cases fail, including the unchanged-image cache hash case. Source restored and formatted afterward. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-path-payload-negative.log.\n\nThe expanded controls caught zero-area line contours producing half-alpha pixels on their diagonal. Preparation now removes contours whose quantized screen points are all collinear, without rejecting self-intersecting contours merely because their signed area is zero. One native-entry fixture also sampled exactly on a circle edge; that outside sample was corrected from y=30 to y=25 rather than changing antialiasing.\n\nClipData retains the push-time affine and fill rule. Path rows are flattened lazily once per pushed clip, only when a raw image uses the selected path. This avoids adding tessellation work to non-image draws that retain their old fallback. Cubic/quadratic curves and SVG arcs are converted in f64 and flattened in physical screen coordinates at 0.25-pixel tolerance, then quantized into the existing image clip format. Open filled contours are explicitly closed and separated. Each image owns its copied rows; existing tight-origin translation and scratch hashing already handle them. Rectangular scissors are retained on raw-image path draws. No shader, GPU primitive/image-instance layout or shared ClipPath/CSS enum change.\n\nPreparation fails closed for non-finite or near-singular clip transforms, non-finite path data, numerically unsafe screen coordinates, or excess complexity. Screen coordinates have absolute magnitude below 1e18 so f32 distance/crossing products remain finite. The input-command and output-point limits are 65,536; output counts include closing points and separators. Empty/collinear/invalid/over-budget paths use a zero-coverage payload. Structural maximum payload is eight header rows plus 32,768 point rows (524,416 bytes) per prepared clip copy; each emitted image retains its own copy, and nonzero tight origins copy rows for translation. These are structural bounds, not allocator or timing measurements.\n\nOther native primitives retain their existing ClipShape::Path fallback. Clip selection is still the topmost nonrectangular shape plus rectangular scissor; arbitrary stacked intersections and fade behavior are unchanged. Shared ClipPath fill-rule/CSS changes remain owner-gated. No browser/mobile pixels or performance benchmarks are claimed.","files":null}]}